WEBVTT

c1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.800
How can a square on a label hold a message?

c2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.900
Look closely at a QR code.

c3
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.325
Its light and dark squares form a grid,

c4
00:00:07.325 --> 00:00:09.400
with information arranged across rows and

c5
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.100
columns.

c6
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:10.825
First,

c7
00:00:10.825 --> 00:00:13.650
the scanner finds three large corner patterns.

c8
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.525
They show where the code is and which way it faces.

c9
00:00:16.525 --> 00:00:19.075
Alternating timing marks help the scanner line up

c10
00:00:19.075 --> 00:00:20.275
with the little squares.

c11
00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:21.725
Once it has the grid,

c12
00:00:21.725 --> 00:00:24.425
the reader can interpret the encoded data.

c13
00:00:24.425 --> 00:00:27.050
Some of those squares carry extra information for

c14
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.175
error correction,

c15
00:00:28.175 --> 00:00:30.850
which can help recover a message even if part of the

c16
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:31.750
code is damaged.

